---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SectionIntro from "../../components/SectionIntro.astro";
import { classicsCards } from "../../data/content";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Chinese Classics | HerbGuide"
  description="Introductory guides to the major Chinese medical classics that shape herb and food therapy traditions."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Chinese Classics</p>
      <h1>Where the tradition comes from</h1>
      <p>
        These pages give readers cultural context without turning the site into a dense
        academic archive.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionIntro
        title="Foundational reading"
        text="Each introduction answers what the text is, why it matters, and what modern readers can still learn from it."
      />
      <div class="card-grid">
        {classicsCards.map((card) => (
          <article class="card">
            <h2>{card.title}</h2>
            <p>{card.text}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell stack">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Foundational text</p>
        <h2>Huangdi Neijing</h2>
        <p>
          The <em>Huangdi Neijing</em>, often translated as the <em>Yellow Emperor's Inner
          Canon</em>, is one of the most important classical sources behind Chinese medicine.
          It is not a beginner handbook in the modern sense. Instead, it lays out core ways
          of thinking about the body, the seasons, lifestyle, emotion, climate, and balance.
        </p>
        <p>
          Modern readers usually encounter its influence indirectly. Ideas like harmony with
          the seasons, prevention before crisis, and the relationship between food, climate,
          and daily habits all echo the worldview of the <em>Neijing</em>.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Herbal encyclopedia</p>
        <h2>Bencao Gangmu</h2>
        <p>
          The <em>Bencao Gangmu</em>, often called the <em>Compendium of Materia Medica</em>,
          is the best-known great herbal encyclopedia in Chinese history. Compiled by Li
          Shizhen in the Ming dynasty, it gathered botanical, medicinal, culinary, and
          observational knowledge into a huge reference work.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a site like HerbGuide, this book matters because it reminds readers that herb
          knowledge in China was never only about pills or prescriptions. It also lived in
          fields, kitchens, marketplaces, farming knowledge, and daily life.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Formula tradition</p>
        <h2>Shanghan Lun</h2>
        <p>
          The <em>Shanghan Lun</em>, often translated as the <em>Treatise on Cold Damage</em>,
          is central to classical formula thinking. It is famous not because it lists random
          herbs, but because it organizes patterns, stages, and herbal responses in a
          systematic way.
        </p>
        <p>
          Even when HerbGuide is only publishing educational herb pages, the shadow of this
          text is still present. It helps explain why Chinese medicine often cares so much
          about pattern differentiation, context, timing, and combinations rather than
          treating one herb like a universal answer.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>Why these books matter to a modern English reader</h2>
        <p>
          Most readers do not need to study classical Chinese to benefit from these texts.
          What matters is understanding that Chinese herb language comes from a real
          intellectual tradition with its own internal logic, not from random internet
          wellness trends.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the site explains why goji berry is considered neutral, why chrysanthemum is
          often described as cooling, or why astragalus appears in seasonal soups, that
          language belongs to a longer historical conversation.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>How HerbGuide will use the classics</h2>
        <p>
          HerbGuide will use the classics as background context, not as decoration. We will
          draw on them to explain terminology, cultural lineage, and traditional frameworks.
          We will not pretend that quoting a classic automatically proves a modern clinical
          claim.
        </p>
        <p>
          That balance is important for trust. Readers deserve cultural depth, but they also
          deserve clear boundaries between tradition, interpretation, and evidence.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
